Following the order of Samdech Techo, Kratie Provincial Governor held a meeting on measures to prevent and strengthen the conservation of Mekong River dolphins
(Kratie): On January 2, 2023, on the occasion of presiding over the groundbreaking ceremony for a bridge across the Mekong River in Kratie province, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, ordered the governors of Kratie and Stung Treng provinces to pay more attention to conservation. Dolphins should not rely solely on the Ministry of Agriculture and recommended the establishment of a permanent core area to conserve dolphins.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's order follows reports of a series of endangered species of dolphins due to fishing nets and some illegal fishing in the Mekong River dolphin area.
After receiving this order, on the evening of January 6, 2023, the Governor of Kratie Province convened an emergency meeting with relevant officials on measures to prevent and strengthen the conservation of the Mekong River dolphins in a stable manner. Illegal fishing in dolphin conservation areas.
The meeting was attended by the Deputy Governor, Provincial Police Commissioner, Provincial Gendarmerie Commander, Director of the Department of Agriculture, Director of the Provincial Department of Tourism, Director of Fisheries Administration, the governors of the three districts (Prey Prosop district, Chet Borey district, Sambo district), the district police inspectors, including Post chiefs and commune chiefs are also involved.
"We are meeting now to take action to prevent the conservation of our dolphins, which are endangered due to fishing nets by fishermen," he said. "It is illegal in the Mekong River Dolphin Conservation Area in Kratie province, and the force must patrol from the head of the island up to the border of Stung Treng province."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also expressed his belief that people will participate in conservation without fishing in the restricted area, but in order to be clear, they must throw balloons and throw solar panels to illuminate the animal conservation area. Dolphins to ensure the protection of this dolphin.
Please be informed that the Global Natural Fund (WWF) has declared a state of emergency on the death of Mekong River dolphins in Cambodia, while by 2022, the number of dead dolphins has increased to 11. "A total of 29 dolphins have died in the last three years."
