Asia's largest capital outflows from developing countries in 2022 since 2008
BANGKOK (Reuters) - Foreign investors withdrew most of their Asian stocks in 2022 since the global financial crisis in 2008 as US rate hikes drew more funds. Go for assets in dollars. According to the Bangkok Post.
According to the Bangkok Post, data from Taiwan, India, the Philippines, Vietnam, Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea show that foreigners sold $ 57 billion worth of shares last year, the largest outflow. Since 2008.
After four consecutive rate hikes of 75 basis points, or 0.75 percent, in early 2022, the US Federal Reserve raised its overnight lending rate by another 50 basis points in December 2022.
As a result of the rate hike, yields on 10-year US Treasury bonds rose about 230 basis points, or 2.3 percent, to 3.83 percent last year, reducing demand, the Bangkok Post reported. Overseas for high-risk stocks in Asia.
Taiwanese stocks faced an outflow of $ 41.6 billion last year, leading regional sales, while Indian stocks saw an outflow of $ 15.4 billion, followed by South Korea. $ 6 billion.
Due to declining foreign demand and worsening economic outlook, the MSCI Asia Pacific Index fell 19.4 percent last year, the biggest drop since a 43.3 percent drop in 2008. In Thailand, the SET stock index recorded a modest 0.7% increase from the end of 2021.
Some analysts expect more outflows, at least in the first half of 2023, as further rate hikes in the United States are expected before peaking next year. According to the Bangkok Post.
