International Analysis: Without the President, what would the US House of Representatives be like?
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - After a landslide victory over Democrats in the November 2022 midterm elections with a narrow majority in the US House of Representatives, the Republican Party has been trying to elect its new president. But for two days in a row, the US House of Representatives failed to elect a new president to replace Nancy Pelosi, a former Democrat.
It's the first time in nearly 100 years that the US House of Representatives has been rigidly elected as its new president since 1923. In the history of the United States, in 1856, members of the US House of Representatives spent more than two months in 133 elections to elect their new president.
