General knowledge: Why in the highlands or in the mountains, the weather is colder than in the lowlands?
According to the experts, the reason why the weather is colder in the highlands or in the mountains below is because at higher altitudes there is weaker gravity than in the lowlands, which causes the temperature to drop through. Height too. In addition, the air molecules become thinner and lighter, leading to a decrease in overall temperature.
On our earth's crust, nature was created to accommodate living things. It draws light from the sun, but when the light bounces back (visible to the eye), it turns into an invisible ray of heat. The reflection of the Earth's surface is not the same as the reflection of glass that emits white light, but its dimensions convert white light into heat rays to make life on Earth more viable. Warm.
Gravity has an effect on extreme temperatures because the Earth's gravitational pull at the Earth's crust draws molecules from the atmosphere, such as carbon dioxide, methane, and water vapor. More importantly, carbon dioxide and water vapor are the best absorbers of heat radiation in the body.
As a result, the heat from the earth's crust is absorbed by water vapor and carbon dioxide before it floats to higher ground, thus warming the earth's crust. The amount of water on Earth, both on the surface of the forest, in the moist forest, in the rivers, oceans, etc., absorbs heat and stores it with it, then gradually releases heat energy gradually, thus causing The surface of the Earth's crust is constantly radiating heat from water.
