Maduro's comments come days after the South American opposition party voted to dissolve the "interim government" led by Juan Guaido, which many countries, including the United States, have voted for. Recognized as Venezuela's legitimate leader after the controversial 2018 elections.
"Venezuela is ready to move forward with the process of normalizing diplomatic and political relations with the current US government as well as other US governments in the future," Maduro said in an interview. "We have prepared for the highest level of dialogue, and I hope the United States will abandon its extremist policies and turn to practical policies with respect for Venezuela."
Nicolas Maduro severed ties with Washington in 2019 when the administration of former US President Donald Trump recognized Gaddafi as Venezuela's "acting president". In an attempt to force Maduro to step down, the United States imposed a series of sanctions against Venezuela, including oil sanctions.
Although the current administration of US President Joe Biden has maintained a formal non-recognition of Maduro's government, last year Washington sent a delegation to Caracas to meet with Maduro and discuss the exchange of prisoners among the presidents. Other songs.
