Samdech Techo Hun Sen advised: "I would like to warn what the governors of Kratie and Stung Treng provinces related to dolphin conservation areas must be responsible, not rely only on the Ministry of Agriculture, this dolphin area must be protected. "A few weeks ago, there were dolphins dying from nets."
At the same time, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also instructed the two provincial governors to set up a permanent core area for conservation by banning fishing, nets and other disturbances to ensure that the dolphin area lives 100 times. Percentages, including the identification of other core areas according to tidal and tidal conditions.
"I would like to warn that this area must be a fully protected area, without exception, for the Mekong River, which is rich in dolphins and some endangered fish, especially the dolphins themselves must be well organized." This is an additional emphasis of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
At the same time, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia also instructed the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, especially the two provincial administrations where live dolphins are present, to organize conservation and protection and ensure that the Mekong River dolphins are not trapped. Mosquitoes, in particular, must not have any tools or factors to disturb the dolphins.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that "there must be a permanent core area in the river, there must be a map so that the people know, we drop the bag in it, not to have all the fishing is absolutely."
In addition, Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that in the work of identifying the core areas in the Mekong River where dolphins are living, the two provincial governments must make specific measures to prohibit fishing with nets or fishing rods. Let the dolphins become extinct.
Please be informed that the Global Fund for Nature (WWF) has declared a state of emergency on the death of Mekong River dolphins in Cambodia, while by 2022, the number of dead dolphins has increased to 11. "A total of 29 dolphins have died in the last three years."
