BEIJING (Reuters) - China has appointed Qin Gang, China's ambassador to the United States and a staunch aide to Chinese President Xi Jinping, as its new foreign minister as Beijing and Washington seek Stable, strong relationship. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Sunday, January 1, 2023.
The announcement comes as the new administration, led by a new prime minister, is set to take office during the annual parliamentary session, which begins on March 5 next year.
The new cabinet will replace the current one, led by Prime Minister Li Keqiang, during the annual session of the National People's Congress, which begins on March 5.
All senior officials and ministers except President Xi Jinping will be replaced during the session.
Qin Gang, a former Foreign Ministry spokesman who served as deputy prime minister, has been in close contact with Xi Jinping for the past decade, accompanying him on his travels. All international visits.
