Cambodia launches tourism curriculum for general and technical high school students
(Phnom Penh): Currently, Cambodia is implementing a technical curriculum related to "tourism" for general and technical high school students, namely in the 10th, 11th and 12th grade, in addition to the existing general education subjects, while in the early stages there are Four schools are implementing this tourism curriculum.
This will be presented on the occasion that the Ministry of Tourism and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports held a ceremony to announce the curriculum for higher education, higher education, tourism, front office for general education and technical high school on December 28, 2022. At the NOCC headquarters.
* What is the curriculum related to tourism related subjects in general and technical high school?
During the ceremony, Mr. Try Chhiv, Director of the Department of International and ASEAN Cooperation and Chairman of the Secretariat of the National Committee for Tourism Professionals of the Ministry of Tourism, said that the tourism curriculum at the General and Technical High School is a supplement to the teaching of the subject. Involved in tourism for students studying in general and technical high schools, ie from 10th, 11th and 12th grade.
Ms. Mathalo Lo, Principal of ACT Private School of General Education and Technology, said that the tourism training at the high school level aims to support the talents of Cambodian students to become truly skilled through capacity development. From high school level.
She added that in the second and third years, ACT has a three-month theory program and a three-month practice cycle until the end of the year. For implementation, the school has partnerships with a number of private institutions, such as Sofitel Phnom Penh Phokitra Hotel, Raffles Hotel, Hyatt Regency Hotel Phnom Penh and so on.
According to the results of the principal of ACT, at the end of the three-year study, students are able to work in tourism businesses, some open a small business related to tourism, some continue to study at the university. Another student, in his second and third year, collaborated to create a travel website and travel magazine for promoting tourism destinations in Cambodia.
Principal Martale Llo called on the government to raise the profile of high schools with tourism skills training, saying "such a high school should become a Credit Best High School." On the other hand, students who study tourism need to practice as well as earn money while studying to support their studies.
The minister added that the program will also provide more employment options and skills for young Cambodians to acquire tourism skills, either for young people who are stuck or bankrupt or young people who have completed ninth grade.
In order to implement the first, second and third year front office tourism curriculum effectively, Minister Thong Khon recommended the Inter-Ministerial Commission to design and implement ASEAN Tourism Vocational Training Program Standards at General Education High Schools. And co-organizing training techniques for teachers and evaluators at partner schools that will implement this tourism curriculum in accordance with the requirements of ASEAN.
The Minister also recommended to disseminate and examine the possibility of increasing the training institutions for this curriculum to students, such as private high schools or training schools of various NGOs.
Please note that the curriculum related to tourism-related technical subjects for high school students is in line with the ASEAN training standards, which Cambodia is a leader.
