Cambodia is in the process of rebuilding a "two statues or monuments commemorating the victims of World War I" as a memorial to the Cambodian-French friendship.
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia is beginning the process of rebuilding the "Two Statues or Monuments commemorating the victims of World War I", a memorial to the Cambodian-French friendship.
In fact, on December 25, 2022, at the National Museum, seven statues of "two statues or monuments commemorating the victims of World War I" were left over from the destruction of Democratic Kampuchea. Metals from the National Museum to the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction to assemble and rebuild the Cambodia-France Friendship Memorial.
On the night of December 13, 2023, Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated in front of the French President Emmanuel Macron in Paris that he had authorized the reconstruction of this statue of friendship. At a meeting at the French Government House, Elizabeth expressed hope that France would not oppose the project.
The "Two Statues" is a large statue of solidarity between French and Cambodian fighters who fought together during World War I and was built in 1925 to be dedicated to war veterans. It was destroyed by the Khmer Rouge regime. After the demolition of the statue, some fragments remain of the elephant head, which is kept at the entrance of the National Museum today.
For Chhay Visoth, the statue of "Two Statues or Memorial Monument", as it is called in the construction period, has three main meanings in the historical context, including:
* Second: It shows the ignorance of the Khmer Rouge leaders who did not value the historical achievements left in Cambodia in the past, especially in Phnom Penh to confirm the policy of Khmer purification. That is why religious buildings and monuments were destroyed during the black-shirt regime's occupation of the country from 1975-78.
