South Korea's foreign direct investment down 5 pct in Q3
SEOUL (Reuters) - South Korea's foreign direct investment fell for the first time in six quarters in the third quarter of this year, government data showed on Tuesday. Xinhua News Agency
Foreign direct investment through foreign equity acquisitions or the establishment of foreign offices amounted to 16.61 billion US dollars in the third quarter, according to data from the South Korean Ministry of Economy and Finance. Down 5 percent from the same period last year.
This is the first decline in six quarters since the first quarter of 2021 due to rising uncertainty at home and abroad caused by rapid interest rate hikes in economies. Large.
South Korean direct investment in North America and Asia fell double to $ 6.44 billion and $ 3.58 billion, respectively, but investment in Europe rose, according to the official Xinhua news agency. Up 58.7 percent to $ 3.36 billion.
Overseas investment in the financial and insurance and manufacturing industries rose to $ 7.58 billion and $ 4.17 billion, respectively, according to the Ministry of Economy and Finance. Overseas real estate fell 44.9 percent to $ 1.27 billion. According to the Xinhua news agency.
