Sad! A female dolphin weighing nearly 100 kg was found dead floating in the Koh Trung area.
(Kratie): Sadly, a female dolphin was found dead floating in the area of Koh Trung, about 10 km below Anlong Kampi, Kratie province, on the evening of December 24, 2022 yesterday. Presumed to have died from being hooked.
The series of dead Mekong dolphins is feared to be extinct in the future if officials and authorities crack down on illegal fishing on time.
According to a WWF report, after a thorough examination of the dolphin's body, researchers from the Kratie Fisheries Administration and WWF initially concluded that the dolphin had died after being stranded. Researchers have found multiple duplicates all over its body and tail.
The dead dolphin was an adult female dolphin, 196 cm long, between 7 and 10 years old and weighing 93 kg, and was the third healthy dolphin to die in just seven days. Only a state of emergency calls for stronger measures to strengthen law enforcement in the dolphin sanctuary.
Eleven dolphins have died in 2022 as of December 24, 2022, while a total of 29 dolphins have died in the last three years. WWF once again calls on all responsible authorities to mobilize and strengthen the law enforcement force in day and night patrols.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, stated that the recent increase in illegal fishing activities in the dolphin sanctuary will cause the Mekong dolphins to become extinct if no immediate measures are taken to stop the illegal activities. This is not.
The tragedy of avoiding the deaths of all three breeding dolphins in less than 10 days is a clear message to the authorities at all levels, this is the most urgent time to increase presence. Law enforcement officers in all major dolphin sanctuaries.
He added that to stop these activities, river guard officers and all relevant law enforcement officers must be present 24 hours a day, once a week, regularly patrolling the dolphin area day and night.
Mr. Mak Ponlok, Director of Kratie Fisheries Administration, confirmed that due to the recent increase in the Mekong River dolphins in Kratie province, there have been a series of deaths due to nets due to fishing nets as well as other problems.
On behalf of the Fisheries Administration of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Kratie Province and on behalf of the district director himself, I would like to request all people, especially fishermen, not to use clear fishing gear. The Dolphin Conservation Authority has banned the use of electric fishing nets, fishing nets, fishing rods, which endanger the lives of dolphins from day to day.
