The following is the announcement of the Police Academy of Cambodia: Kandal Province is the third province to stop open defecation. Tomorrow, the provincial administration will hold a celebration march in Takhmao City, where some roads are temporarily closed
(Kandal): The 11 municipalities / districts of Kandal province with a total of 263,963 houses have completed 100% complete open defecation (ODF) and also received official verification from the Ministry of Rural Development. The provincial champions end the open defecation as well.
To celebrate, the Kandal Provincial Administration will hold a parade with the winners on the morning of December 22, 2022, tomorrow from City Road 102 (at the base of Takhmao Steel Bridge) to the Preah Noray roundabout. Traffic was also temporarily suspended to facilitate the march.
According to the announcement of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Kandal Province, the traffic was temporarily stopped at the section of National Road No. 21 from National Road No. 21B to the Takhmao Roundabout, turning onto City Road 102 and City Road No. 206 from City Road 107 to City Road 102 (at the base of Takhmao Steel Bridge) to Preah Noray roundabout.
Mr. Nuth Puth Dara said that people in every house have changed their behavior by using hand toilets with soap and safe drinking water, which led to the end of open defecation (ODF) in Kandal province on September 19, 2022. Come here. That is why the Ministry of Rural Development assigned technical officers together with relevant partner organizations to inspect the ODF situation in Kandal province on November 29, 2022.
In its five-year achievement report (2018-2022), the Ministry of Rural Development said that so far in Cambodia, there are three provinces that have been officially declared as provinces to stop open defecation (ODF). In Svay Rieng, Prey Veng and Kandal provinces, which will be announced on December 22 tomorrow.
The ministry also claimed that Cambodia was the second largest country in the world, reducing the percentage of open defecation rates from 82.7% in 2000 to 40.6% in 2015. This positive result is due to the efforts of the Royal Government combined with the close cooperation with development partners, foreign NGOs and NGOs in the countries concerned, as well as the private sector.
