In two weeks, Cambodia and South Korea achieved half a million dollars in free trade
Phnom Penh: In just two weeks, Cambodia and South Korea achieved half a million US dollars in trade through free trade, which came into force on December 1, 2022.
The volume of trade between Cambodia and South Korea through this free trade agreement was confirmed by Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce at the meeting and farewell of Mr. Park Heung-kyung, South Korean Ambassador to Cambodia. End of term of office.
In the afternoon meeting on December 20, 2022, Minister Pan Sosak noted the increase in bilateral trade volume between the two countries. The minister said the two countries' trade volume will increase to about 772 million US dollars in 2020, compared to 729 million US dollars in 2019, an increase of about 6 percent.
In the first 10 months of 2022, the two countries' trade volume reached 659 million US dollars, up 4 percent from the same period in 2021. In particular, through the two-week bilateral free trade agreement in December 2022, the trade volume between the two countries reached 0.5 million US dollars.
On that occasion, the Minister also expressed his deep gratitude and appreciation to the Ambassador for his achievements in contributing to the economic development of Cambodia in all fields, especially the closer and closer bilateral cooperation.
Minister Pan Sosak also stressed the need for financial and technical assistance from the Korean government and people to support development projects to help rebuild the economy after the Kovid-19 crisis, including the construction of the Friendship Bridge. Cambodia-Korea crosses the Tonle Sap and Mekong rivers.
The Minister said that the Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia is ready to work closely with the new Ambassador of the Republic of Korea to continue cooperation to achieve more new achievements.
In response, the Ambassador thanked the Minister for taking the time to meet with the Korean delegation and appreciated the excellent relations between the two countries, especially in the fields of politics, economy, trade and investment. Human resource change.
The Ambassador highly appreciated and was confident that the entry into force of the Cambodia-Korea Bilateral Free Trade Agreement and the Double Taxation Agreement would increase confidence and further boost Korean investment and business.
Once again, the Cambodian Minister of Commerce informed the outgoing Korean Ambassador that through the new Cambodian Investment Law, there is much incentive for the manufacturing, processing and automotive industries. In this regard, the Minister requested the Korean government to encourage and encourage Korean investors to invest in the business of manufacturing, processing and automotive industry in Cambodia.
During the meeting, the two sides reaffirmed their commitment to continue to work closely to strengthen, expand and promote economic, trade and investment relations between the two countries.
