Mr. Say Sam Al confirmed this to the participants of the meeting to promote green financing at the Ministry of Environment on the morning of December 19, 2022 yesterday. The purpose of this meeting is to promote green financing and share procedures with other entities / institutions, such as financial institutions, in obtaining an Accredited Entity from the Green Climate Fund (GCF). .
On that occasion, the Minister of Environment stated that Cambodia is in a position to derive this green funding from the world to serve the socio-economic benefits of Cambodia in order to ensure development with low emissions and climate change resilience. .
Mr. Say Sam Al said that the main factors that Cambodia has access to green financing include:
* First, Cambodia is firmly implementing its commitments under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC).
* Second, Cambodia is recognized internationally for its success in promoting population health, promoting climate change education, and especially as an important partner in all aspects of the international arena.
The Green Climate Fund (GCF) has been approved as a financial mechanism of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, which promotes development with low emissions and resilience to climate change.
The World Organization for Animal Health (WWF) is committed to continuing to work with local authorities and partners to end the dog and cat meat trade in Southeast Asia.
(Phnom Penh): Representatives of the World Organization for Animal Welfare, FOUR PAWS, working in Cambodia, Vietnam and Indonesia, have pledged to continue working with national and local authorities, partners and other stakeholders to end the dog meat trade and Cats.
The announcement was made during a campaign to stop the trade in dog and cat meat (DCMT) organized by FOUR PAWS, the international animal welfare organization. Dog and cat meat for public health, animal welfare and tourism in the country.
The seminar was attended by representatives of the National Institute of Sanitation and Epidemiology of Vietnam, General Department of Vaccines of the Ministry of Health, People's Committee of Hoi An, Ha Tinh Provincial Department of Veterinary and Animal Health, EU Delegation to Vietnam, Global Network. Vietnam High School of Health (VOHUN), Asia Animal Foundation, Soi Dog Foundation and other stakeholders.
Meanwhile, Mr. Bun Bun Chheang, Campaign Advisor in Southeast Asia from FOUR PAWS, said that we receive regular messages from Cambodians asking for action against these meat traders. The public has been communicated through social media, informing us of dog and cat meat trafficking activities and asking us to work with the government to ban these trafficking permanently and effectively.
According to FOUR PAWS research in 2022, only 12% of Cambodians regularly eat dog meat, and a 2021 survey shows that 90% will support the ban. The anti-trafficking voice is growing steadily across Cambodia. The trade in dog and cat meat offers little benefit, but it poses a risk to public health and tourism.
