TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan last week adopted a "new national security strategy" that includes a commitment to retaliate against enemy attacks, as well as doubling military budgets to develop offensive capabilities and improve resilience. It aims to defend itself in the face of growing Chinese influence, threats from North Korea and Russia.
The new strategy has been dubbed by experts as Japan's military revolution and the beginning of a further move away from the "pacifist constitution", which has bound Japan with only self-defense forces since the defeat of World War II. Come on. So what is Japan's "new national security strategy" and how will it change the Japanese military?
According to Japan's "new national security strategy", the use of counter-insurgency capabilities would be constitutional if responding to an immediate or immediate threat. Opponents, however, claim that this counter-attack is moving away from the principle of self-defense under the pacifist constitution.
In addition, Japanese Prime Minister Kishida Fumio has stated that his government will need an additional 4 trillion yen ($ 30 billion) in annual budgets and may ask for a tax increase to cover a quarter of that budget. But the proposal to raise taxes has come under fire, and Kishida's five-year military consolidation plan has been unveiled without adequate budgets as his government continues to discuss how to fill the gaps.
IT Security According to experts, Japan's IT security and intelligence capabilities are not yet effective enough, with Tokyo relying heavily on the United States in these areas for launching long-range cruise missiles at targets. Any set. To develop the sector, Japan will spend 8 trillion yen ($ 58 billion) over five years, which will cover information security and space.
* So, is Japan still obsessed with self-defense? Because of its past war as an aggressor and destructive and post-World War II defeat, Japan's post-war policy is focused on On the economic front, security is a top priority, and Japan relies on US troops stationed on its territory under a bilateral security agreement.
