Countries adopt historic agreement to protect nature
BEIJING (Reuters) - Countries reached a major agreement at the United Nations Marathon Summit on biodiversity to protect the world's land and oceans and pledged significant funding to preserve biodiversity in the country. Developing despite opposition from some African countries. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, December 19, 2022.
The most important part of the agreement is the commitment to designate 30 percent of the planet as a protected area for biodiversity by 2030. Currently, 17 percent of the land and 10 percent of the sea are protected.
"It has never been a global conservation goal of this magnitude ... it is the greatest commitment to the conservation of land and oceans in history," the director of the Campaign for Nature conservation told reporters.
