26 cranes show up at Boeung Prek Lpou Landscape Protected Area
The Ministry of Environment has been working with Natural Life in Cambodia to monitor and protect the endangered crane sanctuary. According to the 2020 crane census, Cambodia has 194 cranes. In 2021, the number of cranes decreased to only 164, and in 2022, the provisional report on the number of cranes continues to decline to just over 150.
According to expert studies, the number of cranes in the world today is between 13,000 and 15,000 living in India, Australia, Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia.
Cranes have been present in Cambodia for a long time. If we visit the Bayon temple in Siem Reap, we will see a crane sculpture carved into a rock. In Asia, the crane is a symbol of good fortune and longevity. They are also thought to be a symbol of sincere love, according to their relationship. In Burma, cranes are found in Buddhism.
Cranes are present in three main locations: Ang Trapeang Thmor in Banteay Meanchey province, Anlong Pring landscape protection area in Kampot province and Boeung Prek Lpou landscape protection area in Takeo province to find food to continue living in this fertile wetland from December to February. Every year during the spawning season it moves to the rainforest in Preah Vihear, Ratanakkiri and Mondulkiri provinces.
The Ministry of Environment has also been working with partner organizations to conserve cranes and their habitats, with rangers regularly patrolling cranes' habitats to study the effects of climate change and designated wetland management. . Participate in protecting the Boeung Prek Lpou landscape protection area together.
