The war in Cambodia between 1970 and 1975 and between 1979 and the late 1990s left nearly 4,000 square kilometers of land covered by landmines and unexploded ordnance. Cambodia has been clearing landmines and unexploded ordnance from 1992 to 2022 for 30 years.
On Thursday, December 15, 2022, Cambodia declared another province, Tbong Khmum, as a demining province. In the proclamation of Tbong Khmum as a demilitarized province, Dr. Hun Manet said that in order for Cambodia to become a demining country, two strategies must be implemented, including:
First: Efforts to clear old mines left over from the war: This first strategy has been set by Samdech Techo Hun Sen and must be cleared by 2025. To achieve this strategy, there must be the participation and commitment of operators with all partners, both national and international. Together with the people to remove this is an important factor.
Second: Stop and prevent new placement: The second strategy is to maintain peace. Landmines, bombs, and shells usually occur during wars. Peace is essential to the absence of landmines, bombs and munitions.
"Until there is peace, we can reach the point of clearing mines, eradicating hidden killers, and then we can reduce the risk of eliminating the danger that our people will suffer from landmines and unexploded ordnance," he said. The explosion continues.
As of October 2022, Cambodia has 2,001 square kilometers of suspected mines and remnants of war, of which 703 square kilometers are mined and 1,298 kilometers are remnants of war. Cells also include cluster munitions.
For more than 30 years, the Royal Government has provided more than 200 million US dollars to participate in mine action in Cambodia, as well as support the institutional process and monitor the implementation of the National Strategic Plan.
So far, six capitals and provinces have been declared mine-free, including Stung Treng, Kep, Prey Veng, Phnom Penh, Sihanoukville and Tbong Khmum. The next provinces that will be declared demining under the Samdech Techo project for mine action "Provide safe land, create a smile" include: Kampong Cham, Kampong Chhnang, Takeo, Kampot, Svay Rieng, Kampong Speu and Kratie.
To achieve the National Mine Action Strategy from 2023 to 2025, Cambodia needs a budget of about 199 million US dollars, of which 135 million US dollars for the clearance of land with mines. Ministry of Water Resources: From today to December 20, the weather can be very cold up to 15 degrees Celsius, especially in the mountainous provinces, provinces bordering the Dangrek Mountains and the northeastern plateau.
