Sri Lanka's economy contracted 11.8 percent in the third quarter of 2022 from the same period last year, the second-worst quarterly decline ever for a country in crisis. This serious finances. According to Reuters.
Improper economic management and the effects of the Kovid-19 pandemic have left Sri Lanka short of dollars for major imports of food, fuel, fertilizers and medicines.
Agriculture fell 8.7 percent in the third quarter and industry fell 21.2 percent, while services were down 21.2 percent, Reuters reported, citing data released by the Sri Lankan Census and Statistics Department on Thursday. Down 2.6 percent from the same period last year.
"Our forecast is that it will get worse, maybe a drop of about 20 percent, but now the total decline for this year could be about 9 percent," he said.
Sri Lanka's central bank has estimated that the economy could shrink by about 8% by 2022.
According to the Sri Lankan government news agency Reuters, a number of factors, including high inflation, power outages, high interest rates, a lack of imports and a shortage of fuel and fertilizer, contributed to economic growth in the last quarter.
"Declining demand for goods and services, significant and insignificant due to declining real incomes, has led the economy to decline," the government said.
EU wants to reconnect with Southeast Asia during Ukraine war
(EU) - European Union (EU) leaders met with their counterparts from Southeast Asian countries for a summit in Brussels on Wednesday, looking to strengthen ties. There was a war in Ukraine and a challenge from China. According to the AFP news agency.
Europe aims to boost trade with the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), a region home to some of the world's fastest-growing economies.
The European Union has been pushing diplomacy to push the world against Moscow as Russia's invasion has had economic and political repercussions around the world.
But the 10 ASEAN nations split in response to the Kremlin war on Ukraine, with some opposing Russian aggression and others remaining neutral.
As Europe pressures a tougher response to Russia, another world power is upsetting the summit.
China's claims to the South China Sea have sparked clashes with some of its neighbors and sparked fears in Europe over trade flows through this important world trade route.
But China remains ASEAN's largest trading partner, and many countries in the region are wary of turning away from their large neighbor.
The European Union (EU) wants to be a reliable partner for Southeast Asia's energy-efficient economy amid growing competition between Beijing and Washington, according to AFP.
The EU and ASEAN are each other's third largest trading partners, and the EU sees the region as a major source of raw materials and wants to increase access to its emerging markets in the region. .
EU countries are pushing for diversification of key supply chains away from China as the war in Ukraine highlights Europe's vulnerabilities.
ASEAN and the European Union suspended their push for a joint trade agreement more than a decade ago, and Brussels has focused on attractive agreements with individual ASEAN members.
So far, agreements with Vietnam and Singapore are being implemented, but the EU intends to expedite the establishment of trade agreements with Indonesia, ASEAN's largest economy, and will continue negotiations with Malaysia, the Philippines and Thailand. According to the AFP news agency.
