Commentary: China From Participant to Leader in Biodiversity Conservation
Chinese leaders of all generations have paid close attention to nature conservation, and the term "ecological civilization" has been coined and incorporated into China's constitution as a policy-oriented compass aimed at building harmony. Between man and nature. China's focus on biodiversity conservation is not just verbal, but is demonstrated through practical action and efforts to overcome obstacles to conservation.
Biodiversity conservation has been adopted as a national strategy and should be included in the medium- and long-term plans of all regions and in all sectors in China. Over the past decade, the Chinese government has introduced and improved biodiversity-related laws and regulations and mechanisms to enhance the effectiveness of biodiversity conservation.
Unlike other countries, it is easy to build national parks because they are generally uninhabited, but in China, many nature reserves are populated because of the country. China has a large population. However, the Chinese government is highly committed to addressing these challenges and successfully establishing them national parks.
China has the fastest growing forest cover in the world. From 2013 to 2017, China replanted trees on an area of about 334 million hectares, which is four times the size of the US forest system. In addition, China was the first country to launch a red line strategy to conserve nature and protect endangered plants and animals.
Concerned about freshwater and marine biodiversity, China has also imposed a 10-year ban on fishing in key waters of the Yangtze River and a ban on summer fishing in the sea. During these efforts, important rare wildlife such as pandas, snow leopards, Tibetan deer, hawks, etc. have left the brink of extinction and will be transformed from endangered species to endangered species. Victims.
In addition to its commitment to biodiversity conservation in the country, the Chinese government has actively supported and participated in multilateral cooperation for global biodiversity conservation. The main challenge for global biodiversity conservation is the lack of funding to support developing countries in implementing their biodiversity conservation projects.
In addition, China has promised and turned its belt and road initiative "green." In 2021, President Xi Jinping officially announced a ban on funding for overseas coal-fired power plants under this initiative.
In short, China has so far made it clear to the world that it is a responsible environmental power and a pillar of global biodiversity conservation.
