During the closing session, the Deputy Prime Minister shared the latest updates on Cambodia's efforts, especially the work of the Special Envoy, in helping Myanmar restore peace and return to democracy.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon reiterated the importance of implementing the 5PC Agreement, which aims to promote three main objectives: (1) an end to violence and a ceasefire, and (2) the provision of humanitarian assistance to those affected. There is an urgent need and (3) to create a conducive environment for the initiation of dialogue between stakeholders.
The Deputy Prime Minister recalled the visit of Samdech Techo PM as the ASEAN Chair to Nay Pyi Taw and the meetings with his ASEAN and other world leaders, which paved the way for the special envoy. Fulfill obligations as stipulated in the 5PC Agreement.
The Special Envoy also informed the meeting that he had paid two visits to Myanmar to meet with senior representatives of the State Administrative Council (SAC), seven armed ethnic groups, eight political parties. And other stakeholders, including UN specialized agencies.
Regarding the cessation of violence, the Deputy Prime Minister emphasized that Cambodia has consistently called on all parties concerned, especially the SAC, to exercise utmost restraint, avoid disproportionate responses and stop attacks on civilians.
Amid strong condemnation of the violence, the special envoy also urged armed groups in Myanmar to start ceasefire talks as soon as possible.
Moreover, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon emphasized the importance of building trust and confidence as a factor in helping Myanmar stakeholders to reconcile their differences and communicate with each other in good faith.
The Deputy Prime Minister shared with the meeting that Cambodia has achieved certain and outstanding progress on humanitarian work through the holding of a consultative meeting in early May, which led to the adoption of two main frameworks, including: 1) ASEAN Humanitarian Aid Transport Framework and (2) Joint Vaccination Framework between the Ministry of Health of Myanmar and partners.
Seeing the situation in Myanmar, which continues to be serious, dangerous and unstable, the leaders adopted the document "Review and Resolution of the ASEAN Leaders on the Implementation of the Five-Point Consensus" on the sidelines of the Summit. The 41st ASEAN Summit in Phnom Penh, which includes 15 points to determine the future direction of ASEAN's joint efforts to assist Myanmar.
The special envoy stressed that the release of a large number of prisoners of about 6,000, including senior NLD leaders, key opposition activists, journalists and Australians, Britain, Japan and the United States was a positive move in the right direction. To create a conducive environment for the start of a dialogue process among all stakeholders in Myanmar.
The special envoy also expressed hope that more prisoners would be released in the future to provide opportunities for peace and national reconciliation.
The European Union (EU) commends Cambodia's efforts and the work of its special envoy to promote the 5PC Agreement and reaffirms its strong commitment to supporting ASEAN and ASEAN's leading role in assisting Myanmar. Return to the path of democracy as soon as possible.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon also emphasized that Cambodia, as the chair of ASEAN, expressed its gratitude to the European Union for its generous contribution to ASEAN in this important effort, especially in the field of humanitarian assistance in Myanmar.
