Congratulations! 30th Anniversary of the Inclusion of Angkor Wat as a World Heritage Site
The inclusion of the Angkor site in the World Heritage List is a great achievement and a source of great pride for the Cambodian nation. On this 30th anniversary, people expressed their congratulations with various messages of pride.
Please be informed that Angkor Wat, originally named "Preah Borom Vishnu Lok", was built during the 12th century during the reign of King Suryavarman II (1113-1150), a king from the west (Lavattei). For nearly a millennium, the temples of Angkor Wat have mourned with the Cambodian people in all eras, especially recently under the close attention of the modern Royal Government.
After liberating itself from the genocidal regime of Pol Pot, Cambodia struggled to apply for the inclusion of its Angkor Wat temples in the UNESCO World Heritage List until its final inclusion on December 14, 1992.
To date, the temples of Angkor Wat or Preah Borom Vishnu or Preah Mohanakor Indraprastha Preah Pisnulok have been taken care of by the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister, together with the leadership and staff of the Royal Government are working hard to protect Angkor. In other words, one hand strives to preserve, the other hand strives to spread the word to the world, get to know and visit more.
With this effort, the Angkor temple and the surrounding area are being restored by the Royal Government of Cambodia in cooperation with national and international partners, collecting, preserving, and collecting fragmented objects and reuniting them in their original places with the influx of national and international tourists. Millions of visitors each year.
