Cambodia-China Friendship Radio, Mr. Eang Sophalleth: Participation in reducing the use of plastic as a movement to save lives, help the environment from being affected by plastic waste
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, said that participating in reducing the use of plastic is a movement to save lives, help the environment not to suffer from plastic waste. If all people together do not use plastic bags a day, we can reduce the use of plastic bags by 16 million bags a day and must push this non-use into a movement with providing better choices for consumers. .
Mr. Eang Sophalleth made this remark while presiding over a seminar to disseminate the implementation of policy measures on "Reducing the use of plastic production and strengthening the management of short-term plastic construction" at Phnom Penh Hotel on the morning of December 12, 2022.
The workshop was attended by leaders of the Ministry of Environment, inter-ministerial organizations, partner organizations, provincial governors, provincial capital departments, and many other private sectors.
The Secretary of State stated that the implementation of this policy measure is a new step in contributing to the protection of the environment, as well as taking into account the comprehensive policy formulation and joint implementation of high efficiency. On reducing the use of plastic products and strengthening the management of plastic waste in accordance with the urban solid waste management policy 2020-2030, as well as the policy of safe villages, communes and sangkats.
According to Chea Sina, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Environment, as of 2015, the world had a total of 8.3 billion tons of plastic products and more than 400 million tons of plastic products were produced every year. Of that, only 9% of the collected plastic waste is recycled.
He said that about 8 to 9 million tons of plastic waste is dumped into the sea each year, and this waste will break down into micro-plastics and nano-plastics, which has a serious impact on the marine environment. In particular, micro- and nanoplastics are also a major challenge to human health because they can enter the food chain into the human body and into the bloodstream.
"The types of plastics that pose a serious challenge and need to be addressed urgently include plastic bags and plastic products for food and beverage services," said Pak Sokharavuth, director general of the Ministry of Environment.
The Ministry of Environment has prepared a policy on "Reducing the use of plastic products and strengthening the management of short-term plastic waste" approved by the Royal Government on October 10, 2022, which is divided into three basic measures. The following policies:
First, to promote the production of bioplastics and plastic waste recycling; Second, to promote the use of bioplastics instead of disposable plastic products, and to improve the management of plastic waste, and third: to increase awareness, education, and motivation. And attracting participation to reduce the use of plastic products. Shooting in Chamkarmon district injures 4 people, police arrest 4 suspects for questioning
