China's gold reserves rise for first time since 2019
BEIJING: China's central bank said this week it added 32 tons of gold worth about $ 1.8 billion to its reserves, the first increase since September. 2019 is coming. According to Reuters.
The addition brings China's total gold holdings as of the end of November to 1,980 tons, valued at about $ 112 billion.
According to Reuters, China has the sixth largest gold reserves in the world after Russia, Germany and the United States, with the largest gold reserves at 8,133.5. Tons.
In the past, China has reported no change in its gold reserves for years before the new stockpile was introduced, and sometimes there has been a steady monthly increase. According to Reuters.
