The President of the ACU and the Cambodian Chief of Human Rights reaffirm their strong testimony, reflecting the Cambodian government's genuine commitment to the promotion and protection of human rights.
In fact, on December 08, 2022, the Cambodian Human Rights Committee held this ceremony in the presence of Senior Minister Om Yentieng, Chairman of the Anti-Corruption Unit (ACU) and Mr. Keo Remy, Deputy Prime Minister and Chairman of the Human Rights Committee. Cambodia at the Royal University of Phnom Penh.
Mr. Yentieng added that the factors that led to Cambodia's leading human rights in the region include the non-implementation of the death penalty, the accession of eight out of nine international human rights treaties, as well as four additional protocols in line with Authorization of the Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights and the Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Cambodia.
Senior Minister Om Yentieng emphasized that "these major factors are strong evidence and testimony that reflects the true will of the Royal Government of Cambodia to promote and protect human rights."
In addition, Senior Minister Om Yentieng also mentioned that hundreds of media, radio, television, internet are open and free in the lives of more than 6,000 local and international NGOs. But act under the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations.
According to Mr. Keo Remy, the human rights situation in Cambodia has improved and stood out as the world faces 19 Kovid. He added that the vaccination campaign, the provision of social equity to people affected by Kovid 19, has maintained peace, stability, no restrictions on human rights, LGBT rights and religious rights.
The President of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee pointed out that the Cambodian Human Rights Committee (CCHR) recently visited prisons in all capitals and provinces to monitor human rights violations in prisons and also placed mailboxes at In some prisons in Phnom Penh, the first step is to receive letters from inmates, which shows that attention is paid to the rights of inmates.
Mainland China reports 4,031 new cases of Kovid 19 infection in new report
BEIJING (Reuters) - Mainland China on Thursday reported 4,031 cases of 19 domestic Kovid cases in mainland China, the National Health Commission said on Thursday. This is according to the release by the Xinhua news agency on Thursday, December 8, 2022.
During the same period, a total of 3,767 Kovid 19 patients were allowed to leave the hospital after recovering, the commission said in its daily report.
